      "text": "We need to find positive integers \\( x \\) that satisfy the inequality \\( 16 - 4x > 10 \\).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the inequality:\n\\[ 16 - 4x > 10. \\]\n\nNext, we isolate the term involving \\( x \\) by subtracting 16 from both sides of the inequality:\n\\[ -4x > 10 - 16. \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ -4x > -6. \\]\n\nTo solve for \\( x \\), we divide both sides of the inequality by -4, remembering that dividing by a negative number reverses the inequality sign:\n\\[ x < \\frac{-6}{-4}. \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ x < \\frac{3}{2}. \\]\n\nSince \\( x \\) is a positive integer less than \\( \\frac{3}{2} \\), the only positive integer \\( x \\) that satisfies this inequality is \\( x = 1 \\).\n\nTherefore, the number of positive integers that satisfy the given conditions is \\( \\boxed{1} \\).\n\nHere is the Python code to verify this solution:\n\n```python\n# Let's solve the inequality using Python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the inequality\ninequality = 16 - 4*x > 10\n\n# Solve the inequality\nsolution = sp.solve(inequality, x)\n\n# We need positive integers that satisfy the solution\npositive_solutions = [i for i in range(1, int(solution[x].evalf()) + 1) if solution[x].evalf() == solution[x]]\n\n# The number of such positive integers\nnumber_of_solutions = len(positive_solutions)\nprint(number_of_solutions)\n```",
